Fixed. Root cause in `src/click/core.py` (`Option.__init__`): a flag option whose `default` wasn't given unconditionally got `self.default = False`, so `Parameter.value_is_missing()` never saw a missing value and `required=True` flags were silently accepted as `False` (and `is_flag=True, multiple=True` even crashed with "Value must be an iterable").

Change:
```python
if is_flag and default_is_missing and not self.required:
    if multiple:
        self.default = ()
    else:
        self.default = False
```
Now a required flag keeps `default=None` (and `flag_value` still resolves to `True`), so omitting it raises `MissingParameter` -> `Error: Missing option '--flag'.` with exit code 2, while `--flag`/`--no-flag`, `multiple=True`, `flag_value=...`, `envvar`, and `prompt=True` variants all still behave correctly. Verified with a local reproduction of the described test (`tests.test_options::test_missing_required_flag` semantics) and the full suite: 488 passed, 21 skipped, 1 xfailed — no pre-existing test broken.

